// Package repl is the embeddable Binate evaluation kernel: it owns the
// cross-turn session state and evaluates one complete host-supplied code unit
// at a time, leaving the loop, the presentation, and all native VM bindings
// to the driver.  A CLI driver (cmd/bni), a wasm worker, or a test harness
// each supplies its own registerExterns callback and drives Execute /
// IsComplete (optionally via the RunReadLoop helper).
package "pkg/binate/repl"

import "pkg/binate/ast"
import "pkg/binate/vm"

// Kernel is the driver-facing handle to a running evaluation session.  It is
// an INTERFACE so a driver can hold any kernel behind it — the production
// VM-backed engine (kernel, built by NewKernel) or a test fake — without
// depending on the engine's internals.  State (the turn counter, defined
// symbols, imports) persists across Execute calls; the driver owns the loop,
// the presentation, and any transport.
interface Kernel {
	Execute(code *[]readonly uint8) Result
	IsComplete(code *[]readonly uint8) Completeness
	Complete(code *[]readonly uint8, cursorPos int) Completion
	Inspect(code *[]readonly uint8, cursorPos int) Inspection
	KernelInfo() KernelInfo
	SetPoll(poll @func() PollResult)
}

// Execute runs ONE complete code unit and returns its structured outcome:
// status, the turn index, the engine's informational notices, and
// zero-or-more diagnostics.  Output produced by EVALUATED user code is NOT
// captured here in v1 — it goes wherever the driver's injected externs send it.

// IsComplete reports whether `code` is a complete submittable unit (the
// bracket-depth heuristic a line-oriented driver accumulates with): depth 0 ⇒
// COMP_COMPLETE, depth > 0 ⇒ COMP_INCOMPLETE.  A separate query from Execute
// so a console/CLI driver can decide submit-vs-continue as the user types; a
// whole-cell driver ignores it.

// SetPoll installs the driver's cooperative-interrupt delegate.  The delegate
// takes no arguments and returns a PollResult, so it — and thus the Kernel
// interface — carries no dependency on pkg/binate/vm.  Reserved for the
// suspend / break stages; until a driver sets one, the seam is inert and
// Execute never returns EXEC_SUSPENDED / EXEC_BROKE.

// Error is one setup-time error surfaced by NewKernel (a load error or a
// type-check error).  Returning these as VALUES rather than printing +
// exiting the process is what lets a non-CLI driver (a wasm worker, a test
// harness) decide how to react.  Just a message for now; position / severity
// can join it without changing the contract.
type Error struct {
	Msg @[]char
}

// Result is the outcome of Execute.  It carries everything the engine has to
// say about the turn as DATA — the driver renders it.  Status is the control
// outcome; Turn is this turn's index (the __repl_<n> seed / In[n] number);
// Notices are informational engine framing ("package X loaded", "f parked",
// resolved / cycle / shadow); Err is the zero-or-more per-turn diagnostics (a
// turn can emit several).  Display (the rendered result value) is reserved for
// the result-echo increment and is empty in v1.  Output from evaluated user
// code is NOT here — it streams wherever the driver's externs point.
type Result struct {
	Status  ExecStatus
	Turn    int
	Display @[]Display
	Notices @[]Notice
	Err     @[]Diagnostic
}

// ExecStatus is the control outcome of Execute, carried in Result.Status.  A
// named type (not bare `int`) so the checker rejects mixing a status with an
// arbitrary integer, matching CompStatus / PollResult.
type ExecStatus int

const (
	// EXEC_OK: the turn evaluated cleanly (it may still carry Notices).
	EXEC_OK ExecStatus = iota
	// EXEC_ERROR: the turn produced one or more diagnostics (see Result.Err).
	// A mutating turn (a mid-session `import`) may still have changed session
	// state — EXEC_ERROR does not imply "no effect".
	EXEC_ERROR
	// EXEC_SUSPENDED: the turn's evaluation was suspended by a host interrupt
	// poll and can be resumed.  Reserved for the suspend stage; v1's poll seam
	// is inert, so Execute does not return this yet.
	EXEC_SUSPENDED
	// EXEC_BROKE: the turn's evaluation was aborted by a host interrupt and
	// unwound.  Reserved for the break stage; not produced in v1.
	EXEC_BROKE
)

// Notice is one informational engine message, carried as DATA (like a
// diagnostic) so the driver decides whether/how to show it — a CLI prints it,
// a headless harness ignores it.
type Notice struct {
	Msg @[]char
}

// Diagnostic is one structured per-turn error: a short name, a message, and
// optional rendered traceback lines.  The driver formats it.  (Name
// classification and traceback sourcing are follow-ups; v1 sets Value to the
// message and leaves Name / Traceback empty.)
type Diagnostic struct {
	Name      @[]char
	Value     @[]char
	Traceback @[]@[]char
}

// Completeness is the outcome of IsComplete: whether a code unit is a complete
// submittable turn, plus a continuation-depth hint.  A separate query from
// evaluation so a console driver can decide submit-vs-continue as the user
// types, while a whole-cell driver (which already has a complete unit) ignores
// it.
type Completeness struct {
	Status CompStatus
	// Indent is the open-bracket continuation depth; meaningful only when
	// Status is COMP_INCOMPLETE (0 for COMPLETE / INVALID).
	Indent int
}

// CompStatus is the named outcome carried in Completeness.Status — a named
// type (not bare `int`) so the checker rejects mixing a completeness status
// with an arbitrary integer, matching ExecStatus / PollResult.
type CompStatus int

const (
	// COMP_COMPLETE: a full unit; the driver should submit it for evaluation.
	COMP_COMPLETE CompStatus = iota
	// COMP_INCOMPLETE: open brackets remain; the driver should keep reading.
	COMP_INCOMPLETE
	// COMP_INVALID: the unit cannot become complete (more closes than opens).
	// The current bracket-depth heuristic clamps negatives to 0, so IsComplete
	// does not return this yet; reserved so the contract can grow non-breaking.
	COMP_INVALID
)

// KernelInfo carries the banner + minimal language metadata as DATA, so the
// driver renders it (a CLI prints the banner; a richer host frames it however
// it likes) rather than the engine printing it.  LangVersion is empty in v1
// (the engine does not yet thread a version string); the other fields are set.
type KernelInfo struct {
	Banner      @[]char
	LangName    @[]char
	LangVersion @[]char
	FileExt     @[]char
}

// Completion is the result of Complete: the candidate identifier matches at a
// cursor, plus the replacement span (CursorStart..CursorEnd) they apply to.
// The current impl is a stub returning no matches.
type Completion struct {
	Matches     @[]@[]char
	CursorStart int
	CursorEnd   int
}

// Inspection is the result of Inspect: whether a symbol was found at the
// cursor, plus its rich representation(s) for the driver to display.
// Implemented in Inc 3; the current impl is a stub returning not-found.
type Inspection struct {
	Found   bool
	Display @[]Display
}

// Display is one representation of a value or symbol in a MIME bundle (a
// struct, not a map — Binate has no built-in maps).  A value may offer several
// (e.g. a plain-text and a richer form).
type Display struct {
	Mime @[]char
	Data @[]uint8
}

// PollResult is what a host's cooperative-interrupt delegate returns at each
// poll point: keep running, unwind to the prompt, or suspend.  A named type
// (not bare `int`) and VM-free, so the SetPoll contract — and thus the Kernel
// interface — carries no dependency on pkg/binate/vm.
type PollResult int

const (
	// POLL_CONTINUE: keep evaluating.
	POLL_CONTINUE PollResult = iota
	// POLL_BREAK: abort the turn and unwind to the prompt.
	POLL_BREAK
	// POLL_SUSPEND: suspend the turn so it can be resumed.
	POLL_SUSPEND
)

// NewKernel builds a session from already-parsed source files: it merges them,
// loads imports, type-checks (persistently, so the main file's scope stays
// installed for prompt entries), builds the VM, and lowers every package plus
// main.  It ends BEFORE invoking main and returns any load / type-check errors
// as VALUES — the caller (CLI or embedding driver) decides whether to abort.
//
// Inputs are neutral (no CLIArgs): the driver parses the files and resolves
// the root + search paths.  registerExterns is host-injected — it installs the
// VM's externs (the native rt / libc / bootstrap bindings, which are host-side
// and live with the driver, not here).  There is no I/O sink parameter: the
// engine returns its own output as Result data, and capturing evaluated-code
// output is deferred to package-impl injection.
func NewKernel(files @[]@ast.File, root @[]char, bniPaths @[]@[]char, implPaths @[]@[]char,
		registerExterns @func(@vm.VM)) (@Kernel, @[]Error)

// RunReadLoop is an OPTIONAL convenience driver for hosts that want a blocking
// read-accumulate loop (a CLI, a simple test harness) rather than driving
// Execute directly.  It accumulates host-read lines until IsComplete reports a
// complete unit, evaluates it via Execute, and hands the Result to the host —
// leaving prompt/result presentation to the callbacks.  A whole-cell or
// event-driven host (a notebook, a wasm worker) ignores this and calls Execute
// itself.  The blocking read is the host's (via `read`); the kernel never
// blocks.
//
// read returns (line-bytes-without-newline, eof).  renderPrompt is called
// before each read with the accumulation's current completeness (so the host
// draws a primary vs continuation prompt).  renderResult is called once per
// evaluated turn with its Result.  On eof the loop returns; a mid-accumulation
// buffer is discarded (the user bailed).
func RunReadLoop(k @Kernel, read @func() (@[]uint8, bool), renderPrompt @func(Completeness),
		renderResult @func(Result))
